METHODS — CREDITED, NOT REDISTRIBUTED
================================================================================

Implementing a published method is not reproducing the document that describes
it. These are scientific methods and correlations rather than licensed software:
they are implemented from their published descriptions and cited here as good
practice. Consult the originals for authoritative wording.

  Gradually varied flow, the standard-step method, critical depth
      Chow, V. T. (1959). Open-Channel Hydraulics. New York: McGraw-Hill,
      chapters 3 and 10.

  Energy losses, conveyance, composite roughness, bridges, the deck weir
      Brunner, G. W. (2016). HEC-RAS River Analysis System: Hydraulic Reference
      Manual, Version 5.0, CPD-69. Davis, CA: US Army Corps of Engineers,
      Hydrologic Engineering Center, chapters 2, 3 and 5. A US federal work.

  The pier check
      Yarnell, D. L. (1934). Bridge Piers as Channel Obstructions. Technical
      Bulletin No. 442. Washington, DC: US Department of Agriculture.

  The drowned deck weir
      Villemonte, J. R. (1947). Submerged weir discharge studies.
      Engineering News-Record, 139(26).

  The threshold of motion for a river bed
      Shields, A. (1936). Anwendung der Ähnlichkeitsmechanik und der
      Turbulenzforschung auf die Geschiebebewegung. Mitteilungen der
      Preußischen Versuchsanstalt für Wasserbau und Schiffbau, Heft 26. Berlin.
      Soulsby, R. L. (1997). Dynamics of Marine Sands: A Manual for Practical
      Applications. London: Thomas Telford (the fit to Shields' curve, with
      Whitehouse).

  British National Grid and latitude and longitude
      Ordnance Survey (2020). A Guide to Coordinate Systems in Great Britain,
      v3.6: the Helmert shift and transverse Mercator formulae.

docs/method-notes.html names every one of these against what the code takes
